17.text:
The D1-D2 white chain is in one-liberty atari.

* D1–D2 : {D3}
* C3–C4 : {D3,D4,C5}

So D3 is the unique shared liberty of the two white chains.  
1…B D3 captures D1–D2 at once, hence any first White move ≠ D3 loses the
D1–D2 group on Black’s first reply. The only legal first move is therefore

1. W D3

which joins D1–D2–D3–C3–C4. After 1.W D3

W* = {D1,D2,D3,C3,C4}  L(W*) = {D4,C5}
B1 = A2               L = {A1,A3,B2}
B2 = B3–B4            L = {A3,A4,B2}
B3 = C1–C2            L = {B1,B2}

Black now has the connecting point B2. 1…B B2 unifies B1,B2,B3 into

S = A2–B2–B3–B4–C1–C2

L(S) = {A1,A3,A4,B1}, four distinct liberties.

White can now touch at most one of A1,A3,A4,B1 per move. Every probe is
immediately recapturable:

2.W A1   2…B B1 captures A1,   L(S) = {A3,A4}
2.W B1   2…B A1 captures B1,   L(S) = {A3,A4}
2.W A3   2…B A4 captures A3,   L(S) = {A1,A4}
2.W A4   2…B A1/A3 captures A4, L(S) = {A1,A3}

and Black can in addition answer 1.W D3 with 1…B C5 or 1…B D4,
reducing W* to a single liberty and forcing White to defend its own
group. With best defence Black can keep S with ≥2 real liberties and
recapture every White probing stone on the A-file. The classic B1/B2
A1–A3–A4 net quoted for an unlimited board is unavailable because
White is forced to spend its first move on D3 to save D1–D2.

Hence no first White move initiates a forced capture of any Black group
against best defence.

